course: dictionary
lesson: 10420
title: A warship with three rows of oars
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A trireme was a fast warship from ancient Greece, Phoenicia and Rome. It was made of wood and rowed by about 170 men. The word itself is a clue: «tri-» means three, as in triangle and tricycle, and «-reme» comes from the Latin for oar.
explain: The sentence «Greeks sailed in triremes, fast warships with three rows of oars, and traded olive oil, wine and pottery.» tells you that triremes were fast, that they were warships, and that the Greeks also used ships to trade. Ships could do more than one job.
explain: When you meet an unknown word, break it into parts and look for clues around it. Then swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: A trireme was a long, fast wooden warship of ancient Greece, Phoenicia and Rome.
fact: About 170 rowers sat in three rows, one above another.
fact: A trireme had a bronze ram at the front for smashing into enemy ships, and a sail for when the wind was good.
fact: At the Battle of Salamis in 480 BC, Athenian triremes beat the much bigger Persian fleet.
fact: «Tri-» means three, and «-reme» comes from the Latin for oar, so the word means "three rows of oars".
exercise: «Greeks sailed in triremes, fast warships with three rows of oars, and traded olive oil, wine and pottery.» What was a trireme? | *A fast warship with three rows of oars | A bronze pot used for cooking | A type of Greek sandal | A slow cargo boat with a single oar
exercise: What does the «tri-» in trireme mean? | A hundred | Ten | One | *Three
exercise: How many men rowed a trireme? | *About 170 | Five | Over a thousand | Twelve
test: What was the bronze ram on a trireme for? | To catch fish | To cook food | To hang the sail from | *To smash into enemy ships
test: Which famous battle did Athenian triremes win against a much bigger Persian fleet? | *Battle of Salamis | Battle of Britain | Battle of Hastings | Battle of Trafalgar
exam: Read this: «A fleet of triremes sailed out at dawn.» What does fleet mean here? | A bronze weapon | A kind of sail | *A group of ships | A rowing song
